# Line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#
# eval/corpus/verify.py hashes every corpus document BYTE FOR BYTE and refuses
# to run an evaluation against a corpus whose hashes do not match MANIFEST.yaml.
# That gate is correct and load-bearing: an eval run against a drifted corpus
# produces numbers that cannot be compared to any baseline.
#
# Without this file, git applies its platform default. On Windows
# (core.autocrlf=true) every text file gains CRLF on checkout, so the corpus
# documents a fresh clone produces are NOT the bytes the manifest describes:
#
#     manifest : a54bedf70f18d6689e70826e2b898581ff44d54597e56bcb93acfabf01b469a5
#     on disk  : 9b3fff1c2b1db43b04cdbb2fd08f7f272d2aaf83c28bc76703f3a9b533f4ccea
#     with CR removed -> a54bedf7...  (the manifest value, exactly)
#
# Measured during end-to-end verification: SEVEN of thirteen tier-B documents
# mismatched on a clean Windows clone, so `eval/corpus/verify.py` failed and the
# evaluation harness could not run AT ALL. It was invisible for the whole
# engagement because every prior eval ran in a working tree that already had the
# files, never in a fresh clone.
#
# `-text` disables all conversion, so these files are checked out exactly as
# stored — which is what a byte-for-byte hash requires.
backend/eval/corpus/** -text

# The vendored OCR models are binary and checksummed by api/verify_tessdata.py
# for the same reason. Conversion would corrupt them outright.
backend/vendor/tessdata/** -binary

# Shell scripts and anything executed by a Linux container must keep LF; CRLF
# in a shebang line produces "bad interpreter: no such file or directory".
*.sh text eol=lf
